<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="2d-games-space-farm">
    <title>Space Farm</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>Dodge The Creeps is a 2D game where the player and the
        enemies move. The player can't leave the screen, and
        the only interaction is touching the enemy, which ends 
        the game. In this next game, Space Farm, it is still a 2D 
        game where the space is fixed, but there are no walls or
        enemies. Instead there are resources that can only be retreived
        by a projectile, and your ship can destroy the resources on contact
        which damages the ship.
            </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Example Game</caption>
        <image source="dodge_preview.gif" width="25%" >
         <shortdescription>An animation of the 2D game is shown with a score from 0 to 5 until Game Over.</shortdescription>
     </image>
    </figure>
    <p>You will learn how to make a custom growing area class 
        that can be used by different Sprites in the game. You'll
        also learn about different ways to draw and change sprites,
        some more about physics processing, and adding more
        heads up information for the player. 
    </p>
    
    <p>The game is called "Space Farm". Your ship must move and avoid the
            crystals while farming the biggest crystals you can by lassoing
            them.</p>
    <p>You will learn to:</p>
    <p>
    <ul>
      <li>Create another complete 2D game with the Godot editor.</li>
      <li>Structure a simple game project.</li>
      <li>Move the player character and add a Particle generator to it.</li>
      <li>Make a Growing Area 2D that gets bigger over time.</li>
      <li>Inherit the Growing Area 2D to make a space lasso.</li>
      <li>Inherit the Growing Area 2D to make a stationary crystal.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>And more.</p>
    
    <observation xml:id="2d-games-space-farm-prerequisites">
      <title>Prerequisites</title>
      <p></p>
      </observation>

      </introduction>
